          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The winepress primarily symbolizes divine wrath and judgment, with the wicked crushed like grapes; abundance and covenant blessing are recognized as secondary, context-specific senses.\",\"book_summary\":\"The winepress symbolizes the vineyard’s reckoning before its Owner: good fruit is rendered as abundant wine, while wicked fruit results in the growers themselves being crushed in wrath.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Divine judgment and wrath against the wicked.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A covenantal reckoning of fruit that culminates in either blessed abundance or punitive crushing.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book retains the consensus judgment cases but makes them one branch of a broader two-outcome symbol. It elevates full presses signifying good fruit and blessing from a secondary contextual use to an integral positive vintage alongside wrath.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction: the book affirms the standard wrath identification for wickedness. The material difference is that it denies wrath exhausts the symbol’s governing sense, subsuming it under a broader reckoning framework.\",\"rationale\":\"The consensus and book agree on Joel 3, Isaiah 63, Lamentations 1, and Revelation 14 as punitive winepress imagery. The book additionally integrates Proverbs 3:10 and Joel 2:24 into the core and uses Isaiah 5 to frame both outcomes as the Owner’s assessment of vineyard fruit. This changes the symbol’s extension and organizing category without replacing its central judgment referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The positive press texts explicitly depict prosperity but do not themselves clearly describe a judicial reckoning or fruit being rendered to an Owner. That unifying framework is synthesized chiefly from Isaiah 5 rather than stated across all cited passages.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
